title: What If an AI Ran a Country for One Year?   (AI điều hành quốc gia)
channel: ifm
format: long
run: 1
next: What if money disappeared?
voice: am_puck
people: facet
sources: e-Estonia, AI strategy factsheet (99% of services online, digital ID, 130+ public-sector AI projects since 2019, Bürokratt); Medium, "Digital Public Administration as State Capacity: The Estonian Experience", 10 Jun 2026; Jarnias Cyril, "Estonia Digital Society 2026", 17 Apr 2026 (online voting since 2005, about 51% online in 2023); ASJP, "The Role of AI in Advancing Administrative Management: Estonia as a Model", 30 Apr 2026; AIForge, "Government and Public Sector AI"; The Best AI, "AI in Government and Society" ch. 44; The Statesman, "AI in public service", 2026 (Canada's algorithmic impact assessment); Wikipedia, "Robodebt scheme" (2016 start, scrapped May 2020, 470,000 debts, A$1.8 billion settlement June 2021); Federal Court of Australia, Prygodicz v Commonwealth (Robodebt settlement). The country in the film is fictional and generic.

// 0:00 [4 s] HOOK — the lever, the question types, dive through the screen
# vis kind=ifm act=lever q="WHAT IF|AN AI RAN|A COUNTRY?" clock=off
What if an AI ran a country for one year?
No president. No parliament. Just a machine and a budget.

// 0:05 [7 s] SUBJECT: a plain government building at night gets a new tenant
//   0.0 CAMERA push into the door · TEXT OPEN 24/7 (in the act) · FX flash as the lights come on · BRIDGE scan · SOUND lock, power up
# vis kind=w act=x_arc_keys then="cam=orbit@1|act=servers off=0 ai=1@2" trans=scan clock="DAY 0" dir="0-2.4 zoom 1.35 .5,.6 ease=io; @1 fx flash; @2 text pop 'NEVER SLEEPS' .5,.18 yellow"
Day zero. A small country hands the keys to a machine.
It moves in overnight.
It never sleeps, never takes a holiday, and reads every file.

// 0:15 [5 s] SUBJECT: the promise — one year on the simulation clock
//   0.0 CAMERA pull out to the whole globe · TEXT 365 DAYS · FX shock on "run it" · BRIDGE zoomthru
# vis kind=w act=globe view=side night=.4 arcs=8 then="act=zoom@1" trans=zoomthru clock="DAY 0" dir="0-2 zoom 1.3 .55,.45; @0 text counter '365|days on the clock' .5,.18 yellow; @1 fx shock .5,.5 teal; @1 sfx ifm_lever"
The machine gets one year, the whole budget, and every queue.
Let's run it.

// 0:22 [7 s] SUBJECT: machines already at work inside governments today
//   0.0 CAMERA truck along the server aisle · TEXT ALREADY HERE · FX chroma on "fraud" · BRIDGE whip
# vis kind=w act=servers off=0 ai=1 then="icon:tabler:bus@2 .7,.6,.22 pop\"@1|cam=pull@2" trans=whip clock="DAY 0" dir="0-2 pan .08,0; @0 text stamp 'ALREADY HERE' .5,.18 teal; @1+.4 fx chroma 10"
This isn't as far away as it sounds.
Governments already use machines to check benefits and spot tax fraud.
Some even plan the bus routes.

// 0:31 [9 s] SUBJECT: one digital ID card opens tax, health, voting and business
//   0.0 CAMERA push into the chip · TEXT about 99% · FX shock on the card · BRIDGE match
# vis kind=w act=x_arc_idcard then="cam=pull@1|act=count n=100 hl=99 icon=person value=99% label=\"services online\"@2|cam=truckR@3" trans=match clock="DAY 0" dir="0-2.5 zoom 1.15 .5,.5 ease=io; @0 text type 'ESTONIA|a real country, human-run' .5,.12 paper; @1 fx iris .5,.5"
Look at Estonia, a real country run by humans.
Every resident has a digital ID.
One card opens tax, health, voting and business.
About ninety-nine percent of its public services are online.

// 0:42 [7 s] SUBJECT: voting from a laptop, about half of all votes
//   0.0 CAMERA zoom to the meter · TEXT 51% · FX flash when the meter stops · BRIDGE slices
# vis kind=w act=x_arc_vote then="cam=dutch@1|act=crowd view=wide pose=phone@2" trans=slices clock="DAY 0" dir="@1-@2 zoom 1.3 .5,.62; @1+1.4 fx flash; @1+1.4 sfx ifm_ding"
Estonians have voted online since 2005.
In 2023, about half of all votes came in over the internet.
From a sofa.

// 0:51 [7 s] SUBJECT: 130 AI projects in a human government — our machine goes further
//   0.0 CAMERA push · TEXT 130+ counter · FX glitch on "further" · BRIDGE bloom
# vis kind=w act=count n=130 icon=person value=130+ label="AI projects since 2019" then="cam=truckR@1|act=stage bg=night items=\"ai_core@1 .5,.85,.6 grow\"@2" trans=bloom clock="DAY 0" dir="0-2 zoom 1.2 .5,.45; @2 fx glitch; @2 text slam 'WE GO FURTHER' .5,.16 coral"
Its government has run more than a hundred and thirty AI projects since 2019.
But people still make the big calls.
Our machine goes further. It makes all of them.

// 1:01 [4 s] SUBJECT: the honesty flag — everything after this is simulated
//   0.0 CAMERA tilt · TEXT SIMULATED · FX glitch · BRIDGE ticket
# vis kind=w act=crowd view=wide pose=walk people=holo look=night trans=ticket clock="DAY 1" words="~SIMULATED@0" dir="0 3d tilt 25; @0+.3 fx glitch" then="cam=float@1"
From here on, it's a simulation.
Here's where the machine has to guess.

// 1:07 [8 s] SUBJECT: your tax form fills itself in on your phone
//   0.0 CAMERA push into the phone · TEXT DONE · FX shock on DONE · BRIDGE whip
# vis kind=w act=x_arc_taxform trans=whip clock="DAY 1" dir="0-2.6 zoom 1.1 .5,.55 ease=io; @2 fx zblur; @2 text pop 'BEFORE YOUR COFFEE COOLS' .5,.1 yellow" then="cam=tilt@1|cam=push@2"
Day one. Nine a.m.
You open your phone. Your tax form is already filled in.
You check it. You tap yes. Done before your coffee cools.

// 1:16 [7 s] SUBJECT: the queue at the permit office shrinks to nothing
//   0.0 CAMERA pan along the queue · TEXT QUEUE 0 · FX sparks at zero · BRIDGE drop
# vis kind=w act=x_arc_queue then="cam=push@1|act=city view=street time=day@2" trans=drop clock="DAY 3" dir="0-2.5 pan -.06,0; @1 fx mblur; @1+.2 sfx magic_sparkle; @2 text comic 'NO PAPER' .5,.18 yellow"
The queue at the permit office starts to melt.
By day three, nobody is standing in it.
The machine didn't give a speech. It fixed the queue.

// 1:25 [9 s] SUBJECT: one dashboard — hospital, school, buses update together
//   0.0 CAMERA orbit slow · TEXT ONE SCREEN · FX shock on each pulse · BRIDGE scan
# vis kind=w act=x_arc_dashboard trans=scan clock="DAY 30" dir="0-3 orbit 6; @0 text neon 'ONE SCREEN' .5,.1 teal; @1 fx shock .2,.5 teal; @3 fx shock .8,.5 teal" then="cam=orbit@1|cam=truckL@2|cam=crane@3"
Day thirty. The machine watches everything at once.
Hospital waiting lists.
School timetables. Bus routes.
When one changes, the others adjust in seconds.

// 1:35 [6 s] SUBJECT: buses that come when people are waiting
//   0.0 CAMERA truck right with the bus · TEXT ON TIME · FX speed lines · BRIDGE liquid
# vis kind=w act=stage bg=day items="person_phone@0 .25,.85,.32 pop|person_stand@0 .35,.86,.3 pop|bus@1 .7,.86,.24 slide" then="cam=truckL@1|act=city view=aerial time=day@2" trans=liquid clock="DAY 45" dir="0-2 pan .06,0; @1 fx speed .7,.6; @1 sfx ifm_motion_whoosh_long; @2 text pop 'ON TIME' .5,.18 yellow"
You walk to the bus stop.
The bus is already there, because the machine saw you coming.
Slightly creepy. Very convenient.

// 1:42 [9 s] SUBJECT: the budget rebalances itself; the bridge waits a year
//   0.0 CAMERA push into the bars · TEXT WAIT 1 YEAR (in the act) · FX flash on "waits" · BRIDGE stripes2
# vis kind=w act=x_arc_budget trans=stripes2 clock="DAY 90" dir="0-3 zoom 1.1 .5,.6 ease=io; @2 fx chroma 10; @3 text highlight 'NO BRIBES' .5,.12 teal" then="cam=crane@1|cam=pull@2|cam=dutch@3"
Day ninety. The budget.
The machine moves money like water. Roads get a little more.
A new bridge waits a year.
Nobody voted on it. But nobody can bribe it either.

// 1:52 [6 s] SUBJECT: the same rule for everyone, every time
//   0.0 CAMERA crane up over the crowd · TEXT SAME RULES · FX confetti · BRIDGE fold
# vis kind=w act=crowd view=street pose=cheer fx=confetti then="act=count n=60 icon=person value=SAME label=\"rule for everyone\"@1" trans=fold clock="DAY 120" dir="0-2 zoom 1.1 .5,.6; @1 text stack 'SAME RULES|every time' .5,.16 yellow"
On small things, it gets boringly consistent.
Same rules for everyone. Every time. No bad Mondays.

// 2:00 [11 s] SUBJECT: a benefit application turns red; you tap APPEAL; it goes to a human
//   0.0 CAMERA push onto the letter · TEXT REJECTED (stamp in the act) · FX shake on the stamp · BRIDGE ripple
# vis kind=w act=x_arc_reject trans=ripple clock="DAY 150" dir="0-1.5 zoom 1.12 .5,.5; @1 fx shake .5; @1 fx chroma 14; @3 text sign 'WHO DO I CALL?' .5,.12 coral" then="cam=dutch@1|cam=truckR@2|cam=float@3"
Day one fifty. A letter arrives.
Your benefit application: rejected.
You read it twice. It doesn't say why.
You tap appeal. And here's the real question: who reads it?

// 2:12 [8 s] SUBJECT: this already happened — automated debt notices in Australia
//   0.0 CAMERA tilt down with the falling letters · TEXT 2016 · FX dust · BRIDGE shatter
# vis kind=w act=x_arc_letters label="AUSTRALIA, 2016" trans=shatter clock="DAY 150" fx=dust dir="0-3 zoom 1.15 .5,.55; @1 text type 'REAL CASE|not a simulation' .5,.88 yellow" then="cam=truckR@1|cam=float@2"
This part isn't a guess. It already happened.
In 2016, Australia switched on an automated system to find welfare debts.
It matched income data and sent the notices out by itself.

// 2:20 [9 s] SUBJECT: found unlawful, scrapped, 470,000 debts repaid
//   0.0 CAMERA push on the stamp · TEXT 470,000 counter · FX shock on SCRAPPED · BRIDGE pixel
# vis kind=w act=x_arc_verdict then="cam=float@1|act=count n=120 hl=120 icon=coin value=470,000 label=\"debts to be repaid\"@2" trans=pixel clock="DAY 150" dir="0-1.2 zoom 1.1 .45,.5; @0+.75 fx shake .6; @2 text counter '470,000|wrongly issued debts' .5,.16 yellow"
People called it Robodebt. It was later found unlawful.
In 2020, the government scrapped it.
About four hundred and seventy thousand wrongly issued debts had to be paid back.

// 2:30 [5 s] SUBJECT: the bill — a 1.8 billion dollar settlement
//   0.0 CAMERA push · TEXT A$1.8 BILLION · FX flash · BRIDGE flip3d
# vis kind=w act=money view=cash word="A$1.8 BILLION" trans=flip3d clock="DAY 150" dir="0-2 zoom 1.25 .5,.45; @0 text counter 'A$1,800,000,000|class action settlement, 2021' .5,.16 yellow; @0+1 fx flash"
A class action settled in 2021 for one point eight billion Australian dollars.

// 2:36 [8 s] SUBJECT: you can quit an app, you can't quit your country
//   0.0 CAMERA split sweep · TEXT NO OPT-OUT · FX glitch on "can't" · BRIDGE cube
# vis kind=w act=split a="phone view=pov screen=offline" b="x_arc_letters" la="AN APP" lb="A STATE" trans=cube clock="DAY 160" dir="@1 fx glitch; @1 text slam 'NO OPT-OUT' .5,.16 coral" then="cam=push@1|cam=orbit@2"
That's the catch. You can quit an app.
You can't quit your country.
When a state machine gets it wrong, the mistake copies itself to everyone.

// 2:45 [9 s] SUBJECT: people outside a plain building; the signs ask one thing
//   0.0 CAMERA push past the crowd to the first sign · TEXT (signs in the act) · FX shock on the sign · BRIDGE blobs
# vis kind=w act=x_arc_protest trans=blobs clock="DAY 200" dir="0-3 zoom 1.12 .35,.6 ease=io; @2 fx vignette; @2 sfx crowd_murmur" then="cam=orbit@1|cam=truckL@2"
Day two hundred. People gather outside a plain building.
Their signs don't say "stop the robot."
They ask one thing: who decides the appeal?

// 2:55 [10 s] SUBJECT: a real answer — check, publish, watch, then switch it on
//   0.0 CAMERA push down the checklist · TEXT (checklist in the act) · FX flash on LIVE · BRIDGE fold
# vis kind=w act=x_arc_checklist trans=fold clock="DAY 210" dir="0-3 pan .03,0; @3+.9 fx flash" then="cam=truckL@1|cam=crane@2|cam=pull@3"
Some countries already have an answer. Canada, for one.
Before a department switches on an automated decision system, it must assess the likely impact.
Then publish that assessment.
And keep watching the results.

// 3:06 [7 s] SUBJECT: the viewer commits
//   0.0 CAMERA hold · TEXT A / B · FX on the answer · BRIDGE ticket
# vis kind=w act=guess a="The machine takes over" b="Nobody can appeal" answer=b trans=tunnel clock="DAY 300" then="cam=crane@1"
So, after one year, what's the machine's biggest problem?
Pick one before the machine prints.

// [silence 0.6 s: music drops]
# vis kind=ifm act=answer text="It wins on boring. It loses on appeals." stamp="ASK A HUMAN" trans=ticket clock="DAY 365" drop=.6
But that's not a robot takeover. Here's what the machine printed.
It doesn't win by being smarter. It wins by being boringly consistent.
And its first crisis is one person asking: who do I appeal to?

// 3:24 [9 s] SUBJECT: the machine drafts, a human hand signs
//   0.0 CAMERA push onto the signature · TEXT SIGNED: A HUMAN (in the act) · FX flash on the signature · BRIDGE bloom
# vis kind=w act=x_arc_sign then="cam=pull@1|cam=dutch@2|act=globe view=limb night=.7 arcs=14@3" trans=bloom clock="DAY 365" dir="0-2.5 zoom 1.12 .42,.55 ease=io; @2 fx iris .42,.5; @2 sfx ifm_machine_chime"
Day three sixty-five. Faster forms. Fairer on small things.
But on the big ones, a human still signs.
Because a country isn't a spreadsheet. It's people who need someone to answer.
That's the run. What should the machine try next?
